window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-12-3", "source": "2023年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose the university newspaper is inviting submissions from the students for its coming edition on what in their university impresses them most.You are now to write an essay for submission. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "Among the many things on campus, it is the university library that has impressed me most. While my college\noffers modern labs and lively clubs, none of them stays in my heart as deeply as that quiet building.\nQuiet and well-lit, the library offers a space where I can truly focus and lose myself in books. Surrounded by\ntowering shelves, I felt the joy of learning for the very first time. What touched me even more was a small\nexperience. I still remember the day when I was searching desperately for an out-of-print book. It was a gentle\nold librarian who spent half an hour helping me track it down, which made me realize that this place is warmed\nnot only by knowledge but also by kindness.\nLooking back, the library is more than a study room; it has taught me to seek knowledge actively rather than\npassively. That is why this quiet corner will always be the most unforgettable part of my university life."}, "translation": {"prompt": "改革开放以来，中国人民生活水平不断提高，这在人们的饮食 (diet)变化上得到充分体现 。如今，人们不再满足于吃得饱，而是追求吃得更加安全、更加营养、更加健康，食物也愈来愈丰富多样，不再限于本地的农产品。物流业 (logistics industry)的发展使人们很容易品尝到全国各地的特产。毫无疑问，食品质量与饮食结构的改善为增进人们健康提供了有力的保障。", "sample": "Since the reform and opening-up, the living standards of the Chinese people have improved steadily, which is fully reflected in the changes in people's diet. Today, people are no longer content with simply having enough to eat; instead, they pursue food that is safer, more nutritious and healthier. Food is also becoming more and more diverse, no longer being limited to local agricultural products. The development of the logistics industry makes it easy for people to taste specialties from all over the country. Undoubtedly, the improvement in food quality and diet structure provides a strong guarantee for improving people's health."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "When people set out to improve their health, they usually take a familiar path: starting a healthy diet, getting better sleep, and doing regular exercise. Each of these behaviors is important, of course, but they all  __26__  on physical health—and a growing body of research suggests that social health is just as, if not more, important to  __27__  well-being.\nOne recent study published in the journal PLOS ONE, for example, found that the strength of a person's social circle was a better  __28__  of self-reported stress, happiness and well-being levels than fitness tracker data on physical activity, heart rate and sleep. That finding suggests that the \"  __29__  self\" represented by endless amounts of health data doesn't tell the whole  __30__  .\nThere's also a qualified self, which is who I am, what are my activities, my social network, and all of these aspects are not  __31__  in any of these measurements.\nThis idea is supported by plenty of  __32__  research. Studies have shown that social support—whether it comes from friends, family members or a spouse—is  __33__  associated with better mental and physical health. A rich social life, these studies suggest, can lower stress levels, improve mood, encourage positive health behaviors and discourage damaging ones, boost heart health and improve illness  __34__  rates.\nSocial isolation, meanwhile, is linked to higher rates of physical diseases and mental health conditions. It's a significant problem,  __35__  since loneliness is emerging as a widespread public health problem in many countries.", "matchIntro": "Teenagers and social networking", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "As a parent of two boys at primary school, I worry about the issues associated with teenagers and social media. Newspapers are constantly filled with frightening accounts of drug addiction and aggressive behaviour supposedly caused by violent video games. But even when these accounts touch on real concerns, they do not really reflect the great mass of everyday teenage social behaviour: the online chat, the texting, the surfing, and the emergence of a new teenage sphere that is conducted digitally."}, {"label": "B", "text": "New technologies always provoke generational panic, which usually has more to do with adult fears than with the lives of teenagers. In the 1930s, parents worried that radio was gaining \"an irresistible hold of their children\". In the 80s, the great danger was the Sony Walkman(随身听). When you look at today's digital activity, the facts are much more positive than you might expect."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Indeed, social scientists who study young people have found that their digital use can be inventive and even beneficial. This is true not just in terms of their social lives, but their education too. So if you use a ton of social media, do you become unable, or unwilling, to engage in face-to-face contact? The evidence suggests not. Research by Amanda Lenhart of the Pew Research Centre, a US think tank, found that the most passionate texters are also the kids most likely to spend time with friends in person. One form of socialising doesn't replace the other. It expands it."}, {"label": "D", "text": "\"Kids still spend time face to face,\" Lenhart says. Indeed, as they get older and are given more freedom, they often ease up on social networking. Early on, the web is their \"third space\", but by the late teens, it's replaced in reaction to greater independence. They have to be on Facebook, to know what's going on among friends and family, but they are ambivalent(有矛盾心理的) about it, says Rebecca Eynon, a research fellow at the Oxford Internet Institute, who has interviewed about 200 British teenagers over three years. As they gain experience with living online, they begin to adjust their behaviour, struggling with new communication skills, as they do in the real world."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Parents are wrong to worry that kids don't care about privacy. In fact, they spend hours changing Facebook settings or using quick-delete sharing tools, such as Snapchat, to minimise their traces. Or they post a photograph on Instagram, have a pleasant conversation with friends and then delete it so that no traces remain."}, {"label": "F", "text": "This is not to say that kids always use good judgment. Like everyone else, they make mistakes—sometimes serious ones. But working out how to behave online is a new social skill. While there's plenty of drama and messiness online, it is not, for most teens, a cycle of non-stop abuse: a Pew study found only 15% of teens said someone had bullied them online in the last 12 months."}, {"label": "G", "text": "But surely all this short-form writing is affecting literacy? Certainly, teachers worry. They say that kids use overly casual language and text-speak in writing, and don't have as much patience for long reading and complex arguments. Yet studies of first-year college papers suggest these anxieties may be partly based on misguided nostalgia(怀旧). When Stanford University scholar Andrea Lunsford gathered data on the rates of errors in \"freshman composition\" papers going back to 1917, she found that they were virtually identical to today."}, {"label": "H", "text": "But even as error rates stayed stable, student essays have blossomed in size and complexity. They are now six times longer and, unlike older \"what I did this summer\" essays, they offer arguments supported by evidence. Why? Computers have vastly increased the ability of students to gather information, sample different points of view and write more fluidly."}, {"label": "I", "text": "When linguist Naomi Baron studied students, instant messaging even there she found surprisingly rare usage of short forms such as \"u\" for \"you\", and as students got older, they began to write in more grammatical sentences. That is because they want to appear more adult, and they know how adults are expected to write. Clearly, teaching teens formal writing is still crucial, but texting probably isn't destroying their ability to learn it."}, {"label": "J", "text": "It is probably true that fewer kids are heavy readers compared with two generations ago, when cheap paperbacks boosted rates of reading. But even back then, a minority of people—perhaps 20%—were lifelong heavy readers, and it was cable TV not the internet, that struck a blow at that culture in the 1980s. Still, 15% or more of kids are found to be deeply bookish. In fact, the online world offers kids remarkable opportunities to become literate and creative because young people can now publish ideas not just to their friends, but to the world. And it turns out that when they write for strangers, their sense of \"authentic audience\" makes them work harder, push themselves further, and create powerful new communicative forms."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Few would deny that too much time online can be harmful. Some of the dangers are emotional: hurting someone from a distance is not the same as hurting them face to face. If we're lucky, the legal environment will change to make teenagers' online lives less likely to haunt them later on. Just last week, California passed a law allowing minors to demand that internet firms erase their digital past and the EU has considered similar legislation."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Distraction is also a serious issue. When kids switch from chat to music to homework, they are indeed likely to have trouble doing each task well. And studies show that pupils don't fact-check information online—\"smart searching\" is a skill schools need to teach urgently. It's also true that too much social networking and game playing can cut into schoolwork and sleep. This is precisely why parents still need to set firm boundaries around it, as with any other distraction."}, {"label": "M", "text": "So what's the best way to cope? The same boring old advice that applies to everything in parenting: moderation. Rebecca Eynon argues that it's key to model good behaviour. Parents who stare non-stop at their phones and don't read books are likely to breed kids who will do the same. As ever, we ought to be careful about our own behaviour."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "In the history of horse racing, few horses have captured the affection of the British public like Red Rum. Today, three decades after his retirement, he is still one of the best-known and most beloved racehorses of all time.\nRed Rum was passed around to several owners before being purchased for Noel Le Mar. The agent who made the purchase was the now-legendary horse trainer Donald \"Ginger\" McCain.\nIt wasn't apparent at the time, but Red Rum had a serious bone disease in his foot. For many horses (and many trainers) this would mean the end of a racing career before it even began. For Ginger and Red Rum, though, it was just an obstacle to greatness that had to be overcome.\nRed Rum's true talent came out in steeplechases(障碍赛马). His power, speed and jumping ability carried him to his first Grand National title in 1973. The very next year, Red Rum returned to take his second title. He was the first horse to take successive firsts since Reynoldstown in 1935-1936. Red Rum's spirit and grace had already begun to charm the leagues of Grand National fans.\nIn the following two years, Red Rum lost out on the title, coming in second both times. When he returned in 1977 to try again, he was largely regarded as past his prime. He was 12 years old and not expected to place highly. He surprised sporting fans around the world when he came in a remarkable 25 lengths ahead of the nearest horse, taking his third Grand National win. To this day, Red Rum's third win is known as one of the greatest moments in horse racing history.\nRed Rum was headed for the Grand National once again in 1978 but suffered an injury in one of his heels shortly before the race. He was retired soon after, but his public life and fame by no means decreased with the end of his career. Red Rum traveled all over the country for various engagements. He often led pre-race parades at Aintree Racecourse and was a popular guest at charity benefits and public events.", "47": "In the history of horse racing, few horses have captured the affection of the British public like Red Rum. Today, three decades after his retirement, he is still one of the best-known and most beloved racehorses of all time.\nRed Rum was passed around to several owners before being purchased for Noel Le Mar. The agent who made the purchase was the now-legendary horse trainer Donald \"Ginger\" McCain.\nIt wasn't apparent at the time, but Red Rum had a serious bone disease in his foot. For many horses (and many trainers) this would mean the end of a racing career before it even began. For Ginger and Red Rum, though, it was just an obstacle to greatness that had to be overcome.\nRed Rum's true talent came out in steeplechases(障碍赛马). His power, speed and jumping ability carried him to his first Grand National title in 1973. The very next year, Red Rum returned to take his second title. He was the first horse to take successive firsts since Reynoldstown in 1935-1936. Red Rum's spirit and grace had already begun to charm the leagues of Grand National fans.\nIn the following two years, Red Rum lost out on the title, coming in second both times. When he returned in 1977 to try again, he was largely regarded as past his prime. He was 12 years old and not expected to place highly. He surprised sporting fans around the world when he came in a remarkable 25 lengths ahead of the nearest horse, taking his third Grand National win. To this day, Red Rum's third win is known as one of the greatest moments in horse racing history.\nRed Rum was headed for the Grand National once again in 1978 but suffered an injury in one of his heels shortly before the race. He was retired soon after, but his public life and fame by no means decreased with the end of his career. Red Rum traveled all over the country for various engagements. He often led pre-race parades at Aintree Racecourse and was a popular guest at charity benefits and public events.", "48": "In the history of horse racing, few horses have captured the affection of the British public like Red Rum. Today, three decades after his retirement, he is still one of the best-known and most beloved racehorses of all time.\nRed Rum was passed around to several owners before being purchased for Noel Le Mar. The agent who made the purchase was the now-legendary horse trainer Donald \"Ginger\" McCain.\nIt wasn't apparent at the time, but Red Rum had a serious bone disease in his foot. For many horses (and many trainers) this would mean the end of a racing career before it even began. For Ginger and Red Rum, though, it was just an obstacle to greatness that had to be overcome.\nRed Rum's true talent came out in steeplechases(障碍赛马). His power, speed and jumping ability carried him to his first Grand National title in 1973. The very next year, Red Rum returned to take his second title. He was the first horse to take successive firsts since Reynoldstown in 1935-1936. Red Rum's spirit and grace had already begun to charm the leagues of Grand National fans.\nIn the following two years, Red Rum lost out on the title, coming in second both times. When he returned in 1977 to try again, he was largely regarded as past his prime. He was 12 years old and not expected to place highly. He surprised sporting fans around the world when he came in a remarkable 25 lengths ahead of the nearest horse, taking his third Grand National win. To this day, Red Rum's third win is known as one of the greatest moments in horse racing history.\nRed Rum was headed for the Grand National once again in 1978 but suffered an injury in one of his heels shortly before the race. He was retired soon after, but his public life and fame by no means decreased with the end of his career. Red Rum traveled all over the country for various engagements. He often led pre-race parades at Aintree Racecourse and was a popular guest at charity benefits and public events.", "49": "In the history of horse racing, few horses have captured the affection of the British public like Red Rum. Today, three decades after his retirement, he is still one of the best-known and most beloved racehorses of all time.\nRed Rum was passed around to several owners before being purchased for Noel Le Mar. The agent who made the purchase was the now-legendary horse trainer Donald \"Ginger\" McCain.\nIt wasn't apparent at the time, but Red Rum had a serious bone disease in his foot. For many horses (and many trainers) this would mean the end of a racing career before it even began. For Ginger and Red Rum, though, it was just an obstacle to greatness that had to be overcome.\nRed Rum's true talent came out in steeplechases(障碍赛马). His power, speed and jumping ability carried him to his first Grand National title in 1973. The very next year, Red Rum returned to take his second title. He was the first horse to take successive firsts since Reynoldstown in 1935-1936. Red Rum's spirit and grace had already begun to charm the leagues of Grand National fans.\nIn the following two years, Red Rum lost out on the title, coming in second both times. When he returned in 1977 to try again, he was largely regarded as past his prime. He was 12 years old and not expected to place highly. He surprised sporting fans around the world when he came in a remarkable 25 lengths ahead of the nearest horse, taking his third Grand National win. To this day, Red Rum's third win is known as one of the greatest moments in horse racing history.\nRed Rum was headed for the Grand National once again in 1978 but suffered an injury in one of his heels shortly before the race. He was retired soon after, but his public life and fame by no means decreased with the end of his career. Red Rum traveled all over the country for various engagements. He often led pre-race parades at Aintree Racecourse and was a popular guest at charity benefits and public events.", "50": "In the history of horse racing, few horses have captured the affection of the British public like Red Rum. Today, three decades after his retirement, he is still one of the best-known and most beloved racehorses of all time.\nRed Rum was passed around to several owners before being purchased for Noel Le Mar. The agent who made the purchase was the now-legendary horse trainer Donald \"Ginger\" McCain.\nIt wasn't apparent at the time, but Red Rum had a serious bone disease in his foot. For many horses (and many trainers) this would mean the end of a racing career before it even began. For Ginger and Red Rum, though, it was just an obstacle to greatness that had to be overcome.\nRed Rum's true talent came out in steeplechases(障碍赛马). His power, speed and jumping ability carried him to his first Grand National title in 1973. The very next year, Red Rum returned to take his second title. He was the first horse to take successive firsts since Reynoldstown in 1935-1936. Red Rum's spirit and grace had already begun to charm the leagues of Grand National fans.\nIn the following two years, Red Rum lost out on the title, coming in second both times. When he returned in 1977 to try again, he was largely regarded as past his prime. He was 12 years old and not expected to place highly. He surprised sporting fans around the world when he came in a remarkable 25 lengths ahead of the nearest horse, taking his third Grand National win. To this day, Red Rum's third win is known as one of the greatest moments in horse racing history.\nRed Rum was headed for the Grand National once again in 1978 but suffered an injury in one of his heels shortly before the race. He was retired soon after, but his public life and fame by no means decreased with the end of his career. Red Rum traveled all over the country for various engagements. He often led pre-race parades at Aintree Racecourse and was a popular guest at charity benefits and public events.", "51": "People in business often make decisions based on their own personal feelings or instincts. It is quite horrifying to see people being guided by some unknown force. But how wise is it, really, to let your instincts drive your decisions?\nIn the decision-making process, relying on instincts only makes sense when you have a vast experience to support you. Simply \"feeling\" that something is right or should be done is highly subjective and can drown you.\nOn the other hand, there is a more rational approach to making decisions. Data and analysis have long been associated with informed decisions. These reduce the likelihood of errors and increase the chances of success. Big, systematic data is mostly the foundation of most of our decisions, personal and business alike. But with its extreme usability comes a complication: what do you do if you strongly feel something should not be done, even if the data insists that it should?\nThis seemingly easy question is what drives the need to understand the relationship between instincts and data in the decision-making process. Without making things complicated, the solution to this dilemma is using data and instincts in conjunction to arrive at the best possible decisions.\nInstinctive decisions are always backed by previous experiences or information, which acknowledges that instinctive decisions have worked in the past. Decisions are not about making the choice and braving the consequences because you want to blindly trust your instincts. They are about combining your inner wisdom with the knowledge of systematic data to make the best decisions.\nSometimes, rational analysis and data are impractical to be employed in certain situations. Absence of definitive criteria, and time and resource constraints, and novel situations are instances which limit the practicality of data. The only feasible option is to rely on what your instincts tell you. In these situations, instincts can help you make effective and quick decisions.\nCombining instinctive and rational analysis produces well-rounded decisions. It reduces the chances of making mistakes, and has increasingly become a favorite approach to decision-making among leaders of today.", "52": "People in business often make decisions based on their own personal feelings or instincts. It is quite horrifying to see people being guided by some unknown force. But how wise is it, really, to let your instincts drive your decisions?\nIn the decision-making process, relying on instincts only makes sense when you have a vast experience to support you. Simply \"feeling\" that something is right or should be done is highly subjective and can drown you.\nOn the other hand, there is a more rational approach to making decisions. Data and analysis have long been associated with informed decisions. These reduce the likelihood of errors and increase the chances of success. Big, systematic data is mostly the foundation of most of our decisions, personal and business alike. But with its extreme usability comes a complication: what do you do if you strongly feel something should not be done, even if the data insists that it should?\nThis seemingly easy question is what drives the need to understand the relationship between instincts and data in the decision-making process. Without making things complicated, the solution to this dilemma is using data and instincts in conjunction to arrive at the best possible decisions.\nInstinctive decisions are always backed by previous experiences or information, which acknowledges that instinctive decisions have worked in the past. Decisions are not about making the choice and braving the consequences because you want to blindly trust your instincts. They are about combining your inner wisdom with the knowledge of systematic data to make the best decisions.\nSometimes, rational analysis and data are impractical to be employed in certain situations. Absence of definitive criteria, and time and resource constraints, and novel situations are instances which limit the practicality of data. The only feasible option is to rely on what your instincts tell you. In these situations, instincts can help you make effective and quick decisions.\nCombining instinctive and rational analysis produces well-rounded decisions. It reduces the chances of making mistakes, and has increasingly become a favorite approach to decision-making among leaders of today.", "53": "People in business often make decisions based on their own personal feelings or instincts. It is quite horrifying to see people being guided by some unknown force. But how wise is it, really, to let your instincts drive your decisions?\nIn the decision-making process, relying on instincts only makes sense when you have a vast experience to support you. Simply \"feeling\" that something is right or should be done is highly subjective and can drown you.\nOn the other hand, there is a more rational approach to making decisions. Data and analysis have long been associated with informed decisions. These reduce the likelihood of errors and increase the chances of success. Big, systematic data is mostly the foundation of most of our decisions, personal and business alike. But with its extreme usability comes a complication: what do you do if you strongly feel something should not be done, even if the data insists that it should?\nThis seemingly easy question is what drives the need to understand the relationship between instincts and data in the decision-making process. Without making things complicated, the solution to this dilemma is using data and instincts in conjunction to arrive at the best possible decisions.\nInstinctive decisions are always backed by previous experiences or information, which acknowledges that instinctive decisions have worked in the past. Decisions are not about making the choice and braving the consequences because you want to blindly trust your instincts. They are about combining your inner wisdom with the knowledge of systematic data to make the best decisions.\nSometimes, rational analysis and data are impractical to be employed in certain situations. Absence of definitive criteria, and time and resource constraints, and novel situations are instances which limit the practicality of data. The only feasible option is to rely on what your instincts tell you. In these situations, instincts can help you make effective and quick decisions.\nCombining instinctive and rational analysis produces well-rounded decisions. It reduces the chances of making mistakes, and has increasingly become a favorite approach to decision-making among leaders of today.", "54": "People in business often make decisions based on their own personal feelings or instincts. It is quite horrifying to see people being guided by some unknown force. But how wise is it, really, to let your instincts drive your decisions?\nIn the decision-making process, relying on instincts only makes sense when you have a vast experience to support you. Simply \"feeling\" that something is right or should be done is highly subjective and can drown you.\nOn the other hand, there is a more rational approach to making decisions. Data and analysis have long been associated with informed decisions. These reduce the likelihood of errors and increase the chances of success. Big, systematic data is mostly the foundation of most of our decisions, personal and business alike. But with its extreme usability comes a complication: what do you do if you strongly feel something should not be done, even if the data insists that it should?\nThis seemingly easy question is what drives the need to understand the relationship between instincts and data in the decision-making process. Without making things complicated, the solution to this dilemma is using data and instincts in conjunction to arrive at the best possible decisions.\nInstinctive decisions are always backed by previous experiences or information, which acknowledges that instinctive decisions have worked in the past. Decisions are not about making the choice and braving the consequences because you want to blindly trust your instincts. They are about combining your inner wisdom with the knowledge of systematic data to make the best decisions.\nSometimes, rational analysis and data are impractical to be employed in certain situations. Absence of definitive criteria, and time and resource constraints, and novel situations are instances which limit the practicality of data. The only feasible option is to rely on what your instincts tell you. In these situations, instincts can help you make effective and quick decisions.\nCombining instinctive and rational analysis produces well-rounded decisions. It reduces the chances of making mistakes, and has increasingly become a favorite approach to decision-making among leaders of today.", "55": "People in business often make decisions based on their own personal feelings or instincts. It is quite horrifying to see people being guided by some unknown force. But how wise is it, really, to let your instincts drive your decisions?\nIn the decision-making process, relying on instincts only makes sense when you have a vast experience to support you. Simply \"feeling\" that something is right or should be done is highly subjective and can drown you.\nOn the other hand, there is a more rational approach to making decisions. Data and analysis have long been associated with informed decisions. These reduce the likelihood of errors and increase the chances of success. Big, systematic data is mostly the foundation of most of our decisions, personal and business alike. But with its extreme usability comes a complication: what do you do if you strongly feel something should not be done, even if the data insists that it should?\nThis seemingly easy question is what drives the need to understand the relationship between instincts and data in the decision-making process. Without making things complicated, the solution to this dilemma is using data and instincts in conjunction to arrive at the best possible decisions.\nInstinctive decisions are always backed by previous experiences or information, which acknowledges that instinctive decisions have worked in the past. Decisions are not about making the choice and braving the consequences because you want to blindly trust your instincts. They are about combining your inner wisdom with the knowledge of systematic data to make the best decisions.\nSometimes, rational analysis and data are impractical to be employed in certain situations. Absence of definitive criteria, and time and resource constraints, and novel situations are instances which limit the practicality of data. The only feasible option is to rely on what your instincts tell you. In these situations, instincts can help you make effective and quick decisions.\nCombining instinctive and rational analysis produces well-rounded decisions. It reduces the chances of making mistakes, and has increasingly become a favorite approach to decision-making among leaders of today."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-12-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) base", "B) eagerly", "C) especially", "D) focus", "E) indicator", "F) overall", "G) preached", "H) prior", "I) prompt", "J) puzzle", "K) quantified", "L) recovery", "M) reflected", "N) story", "O) strongly"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) focus\n动词\n【词性槽】they 后缺谓语动词，且空后紧跟介词 on\n【依据】focus on 表「关注/聚焦于」，贴合「这些行为都只关注身体健康」\n上文列举饮食、睡眠、运动后转折说 they all __(26)__ on physical health，意思是这些\n做法都「聚焦于」身体健康，引出下文「但社交健康同样重要」的转折，focus on 语义\n最顺。\n【竞争词】A) base 搭配语义反了\nbase on 是「把……建立在……之上」，需要「A base B on C」的及物结构（如 base a theory\non facts），这里 they all base on 缺宾语、语义也不通，说「这些行为都把自己建立在身体健\n康上」不合逻辑。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) base", "B) eagerly", "C) especially", "D) focus", "E) indicator", "F) overall", "G) preached", "H) prior", "I) prompt", "J) puzzle", "K) quantified", "L) recovery", "M) reflected", "N) story", "O) strongly"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) overall\n形容词\n【词性槽】空处在介词 to 后、名词 well-being 前，作定语，要形容词\n【依据】overall well-being「整体健康/总体幸福」搭配自然且切题\nsocial health is just as, if not more, important to __(27)__ well-being 强调社交健康对\n「总体」的健康/幸福很重要，overall well-being 正是与前文 physical health（局部）相\n对的「整体福祉」。\n【竞争词】H) prior 语义不搭\nprior「先前的」修饰 well-being 说「先前的幸福」既不通顺也不切题，prior 应留给 32 题\nprior research「先前的研究」。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) base", "B) eagerly", "C) especially", "D) focus", "E) indicator", "F) overall", "G) preached", "H) prior", "I) prompt", "J) puzzle", "K) quantified", "L) recovery", "M) reflected", "N) story", "O) strongly"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) indicator\n名词\n【词性槽】空处在 a better 后、of 前，作中心名词\n【依据】a better indicator of「更好的……指标」契合「社交圈强度比体测数据更能预示压力\n幸福」\nthe strength of a person's social circle was a better __(28)__ of self-reported stress,\nhappiness and well-being levels than fitness tracker data，比较社交圈与体测数据谁更\n能「预示/反映」压力幸福，an indicator of 正是「……的指标」。\n【竞争词】J) puzzle 语义不通\na better puzzle of「更好的……谜题」讲不通，本句要的是「衡量/预示」的标志物 indicator，\n而非 puzzle。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) base", "B) eagerly", "C) especially", "D) focus", "E) indicator", "F) overall", "G) preached", "H) prior", "I) prompt", "J) puzzle", "K) quantified", "L) recovery", "M) reflected", "N) story", "O) strongly"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) quantified\n形容词\n【词性槽】空处在冠词 the 与名词 self 之间（带引号），作定语，要形容词\n【依据】quantified self「量化自我」呼应「海量健康数据」，并与下段 qualified self 形成对照\nthe \"__(29)__ self\" represented by endless amounts of health data 指「被海量数据量\n化出来的自我」，即 quantified self；下一段 There's also a qualified self 正与它对照，\n可反推此处为 quantified。\n【竞争词】M) reflected 语义不贴且占了别处\nreflected self「被反映的自我」不如 quantified self 切合「数据量化」之意，且 reflected 是\n31 题 are not reflected in 的答案。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) base", "B) eagerly", "C) especially", "D) focus", "E) indicator", "F) overall", "G) preached", "H) prior", "I) prompt", "J) puzzle", "K) quantified", "L) recovery", "M) reflected", "N) story", "O) strongly"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) story\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 whole 后、动词 tell 的宾语位置，需要名词\n【依据】the whole story 是固定说法，意为「全部真相/全貌」\nthe \"quantified self\" represented by endless amounts of health data doesn't tell the\nwhole __(30)__ 说海量健康数据「说不出全貌」，tell the whole story 正是「道出全部真\n相」的固定表达。\n【竞争词】E) indicator 搭配不成立\n没有 tell the whole indicator「说出全部指标」这种说法，indicator 留给 28 题的 a better ___\nof 更合适。\nJ) puzzle 义近表象但搭配不对\npuzzle 是「谜题」，tell the whole puzzle 不成搭配；表「全貌」的固定宾语是 story 而\n非 puzzle。\nL) recovery 语义不沾边\nrecovery「康复」与「数据说不出全貌」毫无关系，且它是 34 题 illness ___ rates 的答案。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) base", "B) eagerly", "C) especially", "D) focus", "E) indicator", "F) overall", "G) preached", "H) prior", "I) prompt", "J) puzzle", "K) quantified", "L) recovery", "M) reflected", "N) story", "O) strongly"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) reflected\n动词\n【词性槽】空处在 are not 后、介词 in 前，作系表/被动的核心，是动词（过去分词）\n【依据】are not reflected in「未被反映在……中」契合「这些方面没体现在测量数据里」\nmy social network, and all of these aspects are not __(31)__ in any of these\nmeasurements，说「我的社交网络等方面都没被这些测量反映出来」，be reflected in 正\n是「反映/体现在……中」的固定被动搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) base", "B) eagerly", "C) especially", "D) focus", "E) indicator", "F) overall", "G) preached", "H) prior", "I) prompt", "J) puzzle", "K) quantified", "L) recovery", "M) reflected", "N) story", "O) strongly"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) prior\n形容词\n【词性槽】空处在 plenty of 与名词 research 之间，作定语，要形容词\n【依据】prior research「先前/已有的研究」契合「这一想法得到大量已有研究支持」\nThis idea is supported by plenty of __(32)__ research，本段承接前文观点并用研究背\n书，prior research（已有研究）语义最顺。\n【竞争词】K) quantified 语义被占且不切此处\nquantified「被量化的」修饰 self（量化自我）成立，但 quantified research 不是常见说法、\n也偏离此处「已有研究」之意；它是 29 题答案。\nM) reflected 语义不通\nreflected research「被反映的研究」讲不通，reflected 应留给 31 题 are not reflected in。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— quantified 词性也对、又是本篇出现过的词，易误填。32\n空 plenty of __(32)__ research 要形容词，quantified 正好是形容词、又在 29 题刚见过\n「量化」概念，考生易顺手填它；但 quantified research 不是常见搭配、也偏离「已有研\n究」之意，正确的是 prior research——防坑点是别被刚出现的词带跑，回到 research 的\n常见修饰词。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) base", "B) eagerly", "C) especially", "D) focus", "E) indicator", "F) overall", "G) preached", "H) prior", "I) prompt", "J) puzzle", "K) quantified", "L) recovery", "M) reflected", "N) story", "O) strongly"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) strongly\n副词\n【词性槽】空处在 is 与过去分词 associated 之间，修饰形容词性成分，要副词\n【依据】strongly associated「密切相关」是固定搭配\nis __(33)__ associated with better mental and physical health 要表达「社会支持与更好\n的身心健康密切相关」，strongly associated with 是论文里描述相关性的标准说法。\n【竞争词】B) eagerly 感情色彩不搭\neagerly「急切地」修饰的是人主动的意愿（eagerly waiting），不能修饰 associated「被关\n联」这种客观关系。\nC) especially 语义指向错\nespecially「尤其」用于举例强调，不表示关联的「强度」，且它是 35 题 especially since 的答\n案，应留给那处。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) base", "B) eagerly", "C) especially", "D) focus", "E) indicator", "F) overall", "G) preached", "H) prior", "I) prompt", "J) puzzle", "K) quantified", "L) recovery", "M) reflected", "N) story", "O) strongly"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) recovery\n名词\n【词性槽】空处在名词 illness 与名词 rates 之间，构成复合名词修饰语，要名词\n【依据】illness recovery rates「疾病康复率」契合「丰富社交能改善健康结果」\nboost heart health and improve illness __(34)__ rates 与前面 lower stress、improve\nmood、boost heart health 并列，都是积极结果，improve illness recovery rates「提高\n疾病康复率」语义最顺。\n【竞争词】E) indicator 搭配不成立\nillness indicator rates 不是常见说法，且 improve indicator rates 语义空洞；indicator 应留\n给 28 题 a better ___ of。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— indicator 名词性也对，易抢填到 34。34 空 illness ___\nrates 要名词，indicator 是名词易被代入，但 illness indicator rates 不成搭配、且会害得\n28 题 a better ___ of 没词可用；正确是 recovery，防坑点是看清并列里要的是「正向结\n果」的康复率，并为 28 题留出 indicator。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) base", "B) eagerly", "C) especially", "D) focus", "E) indicator", "F) overall", "G) preached", "H) prior", "I) prompt", "J) puzzle", "K) quantified", "L) recovery", "M) reflected", "N) story", "O) strongly"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) especially\n副词\n【词性槽】空处在逗号后、引导 since 从句前，作状语，要副词\n【依据】especially since「尤其是因为」引出强化原因\nIt's a significant problem, __(35)__ since loneliness is emerging as a widespread\npublic health problem，especially since 是常见连用，表「这问题很严重，尤其因为孤独\n正成为普遍的公共健康问题」。\n【竞争词】B) eagerly 语义与位置都不通\neagerly「急切地」表人的态度，放在 since 从句前既无 eagerly since 的说法，也说不通「急切\n地因为」，逻辑不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Research has found the use of digital technology benefits not only teenagers' social lives but also their studies.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】有益于社交与学业两方面\n【定位】Indeed, social scientists who study young people have found that their digital use can be\ninventive and even beneficial. This is true not just in terms of their social lives, but their\neducation too.\n事实上，研究年轻人的社会科学家发现，他们的数字使用可以富有创造性、甚至有益。这不\n仅体现在他们的社交生活上，也体现在他们的教育上。\n【改写】benefits ↔ beneficial\n名词与形容词同源\ntheir studies ↔ their education too\nstudies = education 同义\n【辨邻】 H H 段讲 student essays have blossomed in size and complexity，只是教育/写作这\n一面的好处，完全没有谈社交生活，配不上陈述句的双重落点。\nComputers have vastly increased the ability of students to gather information", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "It is urgent that schools teach kids how to verify online information.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】学校教核查网上信息\n【定位】And studies show that pupils don't fact-check information online—\"smart searching\" is a skill\nschools need to teach urgently.\n研究还显示，学生不会核查网上信息——智能搜索是学校亟需教授的一项技能。\n【改写】verify online information ↔ fact-check information online\nverify = fact-check\nIt is urgent ↔ schools need to teach urgently\nurgent 与 urgently 同源", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Students now write longer and more complex essays than their counterparts in previous decades while the error rates remain unchanged.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】论文更长更复杂但错误率不变\n【定位】But even as error rates stayed stable, student essays have blossomed in size and complexity.\nThey are now six times longer and, unlike older \"what I did this summer\" essays, they offer\narguments supported by evidence.\n但即便错误率保持稳定，学生的论文在篇幅和复杂度上却大大提升。它们如今长达六倍，而\n且不同于以往那种我的暑假之类的作文，它们能用证据支撑论点。\n【改写】longer and more complex essays ↔ blossomed in size and complexity\n篇幅复杂度概括对应\nthe error rates remain unchanged ↔ error rates stayed stable\nunchanged = stayed stable\n【辨邻】 G G 段结论是错误率 virtually identical to today，只对上错误率不变这一半，没有讲论\n文更长更复杂，缺了陈述句的核心一半。\nshe found that they were virtually identical to today", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Newspaper reports of teenagers give a false picture of their behaviour.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】报纸给出失真画面\n【定位】But even when these accounts touch on real concerns, they do not really reflect the great mass\nof everyday teenage social behaviour: the online chat, the texting, the surfing, and the\nemergence of a new teenage sphere that is conducted digitally.\n但即便这些报道触及真实的担忧，它们也并未真正反映绝大多数青少年的日常社交行为：在\n线聊天、发短信、上网冲浪，以及一个以数字方式进行的新青少年领域的兴起。\n【改写】Newspaper reports ↔ Newspapers\n同词指代报道\ngive a false picture of their behaviour ↔\ndo not really reflect the great mass of everyday teenage social behaviour\n假象 = 未真实反映", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Parents are advised to mind their own digital behaviour and set a good example for their kids.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】家长注意自身行为做榜样\n【定位】Rebecca Eynon argues that it's key to model good behaviour. Parents who stare non-stop at\ntheir phones and don't read books are likely to breed kids who will do the same. As ever, we\nought to be careful about our own behaviour.\nRebecca Eynon 认为以身作则至关重要。整天盯着手机、不读书的家长，很可能养出同样\n的孩子。一如既往，我们应当注意自己的行为。\n【改写】set a good example for their kids ↔ model good behaviour\n做榜样同义\nmind their own digital behaviour ↔ careful about our own behaviour\n注意自身行为同义\n【辨邻】 L L 段说 parents still need to set firm boundaries，是约束孩子的行为，而陈述句要\n的是家长注意自身、做榜样，两者落点不同。\nparents still need to set firm boundaries around it", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Contrary to parents' belief, kids try hard to leave as few traces as possible on the web.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】孩子努力少留网上痕迹\n【定位】Parents are wrong to worry that kids don't care about privacy. In fact, they spend hours\nchanging Facebook settings or using quick-delete sharing tools, such as Snapchat, to\nminimise their traces.\n家长担心孩子不在乎隐私，这是错的。事实上，他们花数小时更改 Facebook 设置，或使用\n阅后即删的分享工具（如 Snapchat），以尽量减少自己留下的痕迹。\n【改写】Contrary to parents' belief ↔ Parents are wrong to worry\n与家长想法相反 = 家长担心错了\nleave as few traces as possible on the web ↔ minimise their traces\n尽量少留 = minimise", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Students' ability to learn formal writing is unlikely to be affected by texting.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】学正式写作不受短信影响\n【定位】Clearly, teaching teens formal writing is still crucial, but texting probably isn't destroying\ntheir ability to learn it.\n显然，教青少年正式写作仍然至关重要，但发短信大概并未毁掉他们学习它的能力。\n【改写】ability to learn formal writing ↔ ability to learn it\nit 回指 formal writing\nunlikely to be affected by texting ↔ texting probably isn't destroying\n不太可能受影响 = 大概没毁掉\n【辨邻】 G G 段说 kids use overly casual language and text-speak in writing，落点是担忧，\n与陈述句的安抚结论方向相反，不是源头。\nthey don't have as much patience for long reading and complex arguments", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Historically, new technologies have always caused great fears among parents.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】新技术历来引发家长恐惧\n【定位】New technologies always provoke generational panic, which usually has more to do with\nadult fears than with the lives of teenagers.\n新技术总是引发代际恐慌，这通常更多与成人的恐惧有关，而非与青少年的生活有关。\n【改写】new technologies have always caused great fears ↔\nNew technologies always provoke generational panic\ncaused fears = provoke panic\namong parents ↔ adult fears\nparents 属于 adults", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "The reading culture was seriously affected by cable television some four decades ago.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】有线电视约四十年前重创阅读\n【定位】But even back then, a minority of people—perhaps 20%—were lifelong heavy readers, and it\nwas cable TV not the internet, that struck a blow at that culture in the 1980s.\n但即便那时，也只有少数人——也许 20%——是终身的重度读者，而真正打击那种文化的\n是有线电视而非互联网，发生在上世纪八十年代。\n【改写】cable television ↔ cable TV\n同一专名缩写\nseriously affected ↔ struck a blow at that culture\n重创同义\nsome four decades ago ↔ in the 1980s\n四十年前换算为八十年代", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Teachers say that kids' writing is too casual, using language characteristic of text messages.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】老师说写作太随意像短信\n【定位】They say that kids use overly casual language and text-speak in writing, and don't have as\nmuch patience for long reading and complex arguments.\n他们说孩子写作时使用过于随意的语言和网络用语，对长篇阅读和复杂论证也没有那么多\n耐心。\n【改写】Teachers say ↔ They say\nThey 回指 teachers\ntoo casual ↔ overly casual language\ntoo = overly\nlanguage characteristic of text messages ↔ text-speak\n短信式语言同义\n【辨邻】 I I 段是 Naomi Baron 发现 surprisingly rare usage of short forms，说的是缩写罕\n见、偏安抚，而非老师抱怨写作太随意，主语与态度都不对。\nshe found surprisingly rare usage of short forms such as \"u\" for \"you\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about Red Rum the racehorse?", "options": ["A) He captured public affection long before he won national titles.", "B) He won enormous fame and love from British people.", "C) He became a myth three decades after his retirement.", "D) He owed his great success to several well-known horse trainers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about Red Rum the racehorse 问对赛马的整体了\n解，落点仍是文中可直接找到的客观陈述，而非要求读者跨段推断或归纳全文主旨。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the passage about Red Rum the racehorse 主干是 What do\nwe learn，介词短语 from the passage 表来源，about Red Rum the racehorse 表对\n象，其中 the racehorse 是 Red Rum 的同位语，仅起身份补充，无陷阱。\n【定位】In the history of horse racing, few horses have captured the affection of the British public like\nRed Rum. Today, three decades after his retirement, he is still one of the best-known and most\nbeloved racehorses of all time.\n在赛马史上，很少有马像 Red Rum 那样赢得英国公众的喜爱。今天，距它退役已有三十\n年，它仍是有史以来最知名、最受爱戴的赛马之一。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nHe captured public affection long before he won national titles 凭空添加“夺冠之前\n就赢得公众喜爱”的时间先后关系，原文 P1 只说他广受喜爱，未提“夺冠之前”。\nB) ✓ 正确\nfew horses have captured the affection of the British public like Red Rum ↔\nHe won enormous fame and love from British people\nthe affection of the British public ↔ love from British people\n预判说“他从英国公众那里获得巨大名声与喜爱”，He won enormous fame and love\nfrom British people 是这句话的同义复述，无需推断即可锁定。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 three decades after his retirement, he is still one of the best-known and\nmost beloved racehorses of all time 只说退役三十年后他仍然知名；He became a\nmyth three decades after his retirement 把 still well-known 偷换成 became a\nmyth（成为一个神话/传说），属于程度与性质的偷换。\nD) 以偏概全\nHe owed his great success to several well-known horse trainers 说“他把成功归因\n于多位知名教练”；但原文 The agent who made the purchase was the now-\nlegendary horse trainer Donald \"Ginger\" McCain 全文只突出一位教练 Ginger，没\n有 several。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What did the serious bone disease in Red Rum's foot mean to himself and his trainer?", "options": ["A) It was simply a hindrance they had to get over to excel.", "B) It was surely a disadvantage though not considered fatal.", "C) It was actually the end of a racing career that had just begun.", "D) It was really a major obstacle hard to overcome on their own."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干问 the serious bone disease 对二者意味着什么，答案是一句可直接从原文复制的评\n价，不需要推断。\n【拆句】What did the serious bone disease in Red Rum's foot mean to himself and his\ntrainer 主干是 the bone disease mean to whom，其中 in Red Rum's foot 是 the\nserious bone disease 的后置定语，to himself and his trainer 是 mean 的对象。需留意\nhimself 在英语里指代上文出现的男性化主语，这里回指 Red Rum。\n【定位】For Ginger and Red Rum, though, it was just an obstacle to greatness that had to\nbe overcome.\n然而对 Ginger 与 Red Rum 来说，这（病情）不过是通往伟大路上一道必须跨越的障碍。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nit was just an obstacle to greatness that had to be overcome ↔\nIt was simply a hindrance they had to get over to excel\nhad to be overcome ↔ they had to get over\nIt was simply a hindrance they had to get over to excel 几乎是 it was just an\nobstacle to greatness that had to be overcome 的逐词同义改写，预判与选项几乎\n重合。\nB) 无中生有\nIt was surely a disadvantage though not considered fatal 加了一个 not\nconsidered fatal 的让步，原文从未拿“致命/非致命”来评价此病；选项偷偷在原句外塞\n入新评价。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 For many horses (and many trainers) this would mean the end of a racing\ncareer before it even began 用虚拟 would mean 描述“对很多马而言”的假设结果；It\nwas actually the end of a racing career that had just begun 把这种他人假设包装成\nhimself and his trainer 身上的事实，与原文相反。\nD) 过度推断\nIt was really a major obstacle hard to overcome on their own 在 obstacle 之外加\n了 hard to overcome on their own 这种“难以靠自己克服”的修饰，原文 had to be\novercome 只说“必须翻越”，未评价难度，也未提是否需要外援。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the author say Red Rum did with his power, speed and jumping ability?", "options": ["A) He won his first Grand National title at 12.", "B) He took two firsts successively in 1935-1936.", "C) He surprised sporting fans worldwide in 1973.", "D) He took three Grand National wins in the 1970s."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say Red Rum did with his power, speed and jumping ability 问\n凭这三种能力做成了什么，原文里必有承接句直接给出结果。\n【拆句】What does the author say Red Rum did with his power, speed and jumping ability 句\n中 the author say 是插入性主语从句框架，真正的问题核心是 Red Rum did what with\nhis power, speed and jumping ability，介词 with 引出工具。基础薄弱的考生容易被\ndoes the author say 带偏去找作者观点，实质仍是问赛马本身的行为。\n【定位】Red Rum's true talent came out in steeplechases. His power, speed and jumping ability\ncarried him to his first Grand National title in 1973. The very next year, Red Rum returned to\ntake his second title. He was the first horse to take successive firsts since Reynoldstown in\n1935-1936.\nRed Rum 真正的才华在障碍赛中绽放。他的力量、速度与跳跃能力把他带向 1973 年第一\n座全国大赛冠军。次年他再度归来，拿下第二冠，成为自 1935-1936 年 Reynoldstown 之\n后第一匹蝉联的马。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nHe won his first Grand National title at 12 把首冠年龄定为 12 岁；但 his first\nGrand National title in 1973 是首冠时间，而 He was 12 years old 是 1977 年第三冠\n时的年龄，选项把第三冠的年龄安到首冠头上。\nB) 张冠李戴\nHe took two firsts successively in 1935-1936 把 1935-1936 连冠归到 Red Rum 名\n下，但原文 He was the first horse to take successive firsts since Reynoldstown in\n1935-1936 明确 1935-1936 的连冠属于另一匹马 Reynoldstown。\nC) 张冠李戴\nHe surprised sporting fans worldwide in 1973 把“worldwide 惊讶”与 1973 挂钩，\n但原文 He surprised sporting fans around the world 明确发生在 1977 年第三冠那\n次，与 in 1973 无关。\nD) ✓ 正确\nhis first Grand National title in 1973 ↔ three Grand National wins in the 1970s\nthe first horse to take successive firsts ↔ three Grand National wins in the 1970s\ntaking his third Grand National win ↔ three Grand National wins in the 1970s\n把 1973、1974、1977 三场冠军合并，正好对应 three Grand National wins in the\n1970s，全部时间都落在 1970 年代。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What did people generally think of Red Rum when he returned to the racecourse in 1977?", "options": ["A) He had already passed the peak of his racing life.", "B) He would have no rival in Grand National steeplechases.", "C) He had lost his charm with the leagues of Grand National fans.", "D) He could be expected to repeat his glory in the prime of his career."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What did people generally think of Red Rum when he returned to the racecourse in\n1977 问的是原文中直接表述过的公众看法，不需要跨段推断。\n【拆句】What did people generally think of Red Rum when he returned to the racecourse in\n1977 主干 What did people think of X，时间状语从句 when he returned to the\nracecourse in 1977 用一般过去时锁定 1977 那次复出。考生注意 think of 在此是“对……\n有何看法”，不是“想到”。\n【定位】When he returned in 1977 to try again, he was largely regarded as past his prime. He was 12\nyears old and not expected to place highly.\n1977 年他再度归来尝试时，普遍被认为已过巅峰；他已 12 岁，不被看好会拿到好名次。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwas largely regarded as past his prime ↔ He had already passed the peak of his racing life\n原文 past his prime 与选项 passed the peak of his racing life 是同义改写，且 was\nlargely regarded as 完整对应题干 people generally think。\nB) 与原文相反\nHe would have no rival in Grand National steeplechases 说他在大赛中无敌手；原\n文 he was largely regarded as past his prime 表达的是相反的预期——大家觉得他过\n气了。\nC) 与原文相反\nHe had lost his charm with the leagues of Grand National fans 说他对大赛粉丝失\n去了魅力；但原文 Red Rum's spirit and grace had already begun to charm the\nleagues of Grand National fans 与 P5 处都未出现魅力消失的描述，反而 his public\nlife and fame by no means decreased 进一步否定这一说法。\nD) 与原文相反\nHe could be expected to repeat his glory in the prime of his career 说“大家期待他\n在巅峰期再现辉煌”；但原文 not expected to place highly 明说他不被看好，且 past\nhis prime 已说明他不在巅峰。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What became of Red Rum after his career ended?", "options": ["A) He suffered from severe pain in a heel.", "B) He spent almost all his time traveling.", "C) He lived on various charity benefits.", "D) He remained famous and popular."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What became of Red Rum after his career ended 问退役后的整体情况，原文末段直接\n给出概括语句，无需跨段归纳。\n【拆句】What became of Red Rum after his career ended 主干 What became of Red Rum，\nbecome of 是固定搭配，意为“……后来怎样了”，时间状语从句 after his career ended 限\n定时间范围。考生容易把 became 理解成“成为”而读不懂句子，记住 became of 是整体短\n语，不可拆。\n【定位】He was retired soon after, but his public life and fame by no means decreased with the end\nof his career. Red Rum traveled all over the country for various engagements. He often\nled pre-race parades at Aintree Racecourse and was a popular guest at charity benefits and\npublic events.\n他随即退役，但他的公众生活与名气并未随职业结束而减退。他全国奔波出席各种活动，常\n在 Aintree 赛马场领跑赛前游行，也是慈善活动与公众活动上的常客。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nHe suffered from severe pain in a heel 把蹄部受伤说成退役后的状态；原文 suffered\nan injury in one of his heels shortly before the race. He was retired soon after 明\n示受伤在退役前，且未提持续“severe pain”。\nB) 过度推断\nHe spent almost all his time traveling 把 traveled all over the country for various\nengagements 夸大为“几乎所有时间都在旅行”，原文只说他经常奔波出席活动，没说\n“几乎所有时间”。\nC) 偷换概念\nHe lived on various charity benefits 中 lived on 意为“靠……生活”，把 was a\npopular guest at charity benefits 这种“出席慈善活动”误读为“靠慈善活动养活”，性质\n完全不同。\nD) ✓ 正确\nhis public life and fame by no means decreased ↔ He remained famous and popular\nRed Rum traveled all over the country for various engagements ↔\nHe remained famous and popular\nP6 既有总括 by no means decreased 又有具体 popular guest，二者共同指向 He\nremained famous and popular。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say businesspeople often do?", "options": ["A) Strive to make sense of some unknown forces.", "B) Avoid being driven by their own feeling.", "C) Draw on a vast wealth of past experience.", "D) Rely on instincts in decision-making."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say businesspeople often do? 问的是「商人通常做什么」这一可\n在原文逐字核实的客观行为，不含 infer/imply。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What does the author say businesspeople often do?，what 作宾语前置；\nthe author say 是插入成分，可剥离；剥离后等价于「Businesspeople often do what?」\n结构平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】People in business often make decisions based on their own personal feelings or instincts.\n商界人士常常基于自己的个人感觉或直觉做决策。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文用 unknown force 比喻直觉的神秘性，并未让商人「弄清未知力量」；Strive to\nmake sense of some unknown forces 凭空给出动作。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文说商人「常凭感觉决策」，Avoid being driven by their own feeling 却说「避免\n被感觉驱动」，完全反向。\nC) 张冠李戴\nrelying on instincts only makes sense when you have a vast experience to\nsupport you 是作者给「合理使用直觉」开的条件，并非商人「实际正在做的事」。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmake decisions based on their own personal feelings or instincts ↔\nRely on instincts in decision-making\n预判「依赖直觉作决策」与 Rely on instincts in decision-making 高度重合，正确项\n锁定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What should we take into account in order to make informed decisions?", "options": ["A) The likelihood of errors.", "B) Systematic data and analysis.", "C) The complexity of circumstances.", "D) Personal feelings and business strategies"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What should we take into account in order to make informed decisions? 问的是「为\n了做出明智决策应当考虑什么」，答案是原文直接陈述的因素。\n【拆句】题干主干 What should we take into account，what 作 take into account 的宾语前置；\nin order to make informed decisions 是表目的的不定式状语；句式简单。\n【定位】Data and analysis have long been associated with informed decisions.\n数据与分析长期以来一直与明智的决策紧密相关。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n原文 These reduce the likelihood of errors 说数据与分析「降低出错概率」，The\nlikelihood of errors 只是数据带来的「结果」，不是「应当考虑的因素」。\nB) ✓ 正确\nData and analysis ↔ Systematic data and analysis\n预判「依靠数据与分析」与 Systematic data and analysis 完全对应，且原文 Big,\nsystematic data 进一步加固，正确项锁定。\nC) 无中生有\n原文从未出现 The complexity of circumstances（环境的复杂性）这一表述，与「明\n智决策的依靠」无关。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文把 personal feelings or instincts 视作「常被依赖却需谨慎」的对象，并未把它与\nbusiness strategies 并列为「做明智决策的因素」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What should people do in a decision-making process if instincts disagree with data?", "options": ["A) Resolve the dilemma with previous experience.", "B) Figure out which of the two is more reliable.", "C) Combine the two together.", "D) Prioritize instincts over data."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What should people do in a decision-making process if instincts disagree with data?\n问的是作者给出的具体做法，原文有 using data and instincts in conjunction 直陈方案。\n【拆句】题干 What should people do in a decision-making process if instincts disagree with\ndata? 主句是 What should people do，in a decision-making process 介词短语作地点\n状语；if instincts disagree with data 是 if 引导的条件状语从句；结构清楚。\n【定位】Without making things complicated, the solution to this dilemma is using data and instincts in\nconjunction to arrive at the best possible decisions.\n简单来说，这一难题的解决方案是把数据和直觉结合起来使用，以达成最佳的决策。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nInstinctive decisions are always backed by previous experiences 是「直觉的支撑机\n制」，不是题干问的「矛盾时的解决办法」。\nB) 无中生有\n原文从未让人「比较谁更可靠」，Figure out which of the two is more reliable 是凭\n空增加的取舍动作。\nC) ✓ 正确\nusing data and instincts in conjunction ↔ Combine the two together\n预判「把数据与直觉结合使用」与 Combine the two together 完全等价，正确项\n锁定。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文明确反对盲目信任直觉（you want to blindly trust your instincts 被作者否定），\nPrioritize instincts over data 却把直觉摆在数据之上，反向。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What should we do when facing various factors that limit the practicality of data?", "options": ["A) Make a rational and systematic analysis.", "B) Explore the most feasible options.", "C) Resort to our inner wisdom.", "D) Apply definitive criteria."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What should we do when facing various factors that limit the practicality of data? 问\n的是特定情境下的做法，仍是定位＋复述。\n【拆句】题干主句 What should we do，when facing various factors 是 when 引导的现在分词时\n间状语，省略主语 we；that limit the practicality of data 是定语从句，修饰 factors。基\n础薄弱的同学要看清「factors」才是从句修饰对象，而不是「we」。\n【定位】The only feasible option is to rely on what your instincts tell you.\n唯一可行的办法，是依靠你的直觉告诉你的东西。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文明说数据在此场合「不可行」，Make a rational and systematic analysis 反让你\n做理性系统分析，正好走反方向。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文说 The only feasible option is to rely on what your instincts tell you，给出的\n是「唯一可行的办法」；Explore the most feasible options 把「唯一选项」偷换成\n「探索多个最可行选项」，且把动作偷换成「探索」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nrely on what your instincts tell you ↔ Resort to our inner wisdom\n预判「依靠直觉」与 Resort to our inner wisdom（求助内在智慧）通过 P5 给出的等\n价关系完全对应，正确项锁定。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 Absence of definitive criteria 指出此场合「没有明确标准」，Apply definitive\ncriteria 反让你「应用明确标准」，与原文完全对立。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does it take for decisions to become the best according to the author?", "options": ["A) Applying instincts and data in conjunction.", "B) Assessing all factors when making a choice.", "C) Recalling what has actually worked in the past.", "D) Refraining from trusting instincts arbitrarily."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does it take for decisions to become the best according to the author? 问的是\n「让决策成为最佳」的所需条件，是原文有明确句子可对应的细节，并非要从全文归纳的\n主旨。\n【拆句】题干 What does it take for decisions to become the best according to the author? 句\n型为「it takes sth for sb/sth to do sth」，what 替换 sth 提前；for decisions 是 take\n的逻辑主语；to become the best 是真正的目的不定式。看懂这个句型是关键，否则容易\n把题干误读成主旨题。\n【定位】Combining instinctive and rational analysis produces well-rounded decisions.\n把直觉与理性分析结合起来，能产出周全的决策。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nCombining instinctive and rational analysis ↔ Applying instincts and data in conjunction\nusing data and instincts in conjunction to arrive at the best possible decisions ↔\nApplying instincts and data in conjunction\n预判「直觉＋理性分析联用」与 Applying instincts and data in conjunction 完全对\n应，且 P4 与 P7 双句佐证，正确项锁定。\nB) 无中生有\n原文从未要求「评估所有因素」，Assessing all factors when making a choice 是无\n来源的扩张表述。\nC) 以偏概全\nInstinctive decisions are always backed by previous experiences 只是说直觉以经验\n为后盾，并不等于「让决策变最佳」的充分条件，Recalling what has actually worked\nin the past 把直觉一侧的支撑当成全部条件。\nD) 以偏概全\nyou want to blindly trust your instincts 提到「不要盲目相信直觉」，但这只是「最佳\n决策」的一个否定性前提，不是充分条件，Refraining from trusting instincts\narbitrarily 把单一前提当成全部条件。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2023-12-1"});
